    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 338\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 8686-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  establish  a  commission  to be known as the \"New York\n      seawall study commission\"; and providing for the  repeal  of  such\n      provisions upon expiration thereof\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would  establish  a commission to be known as the New York\nSeawall Study Commission to study the feasibility, costs,  impacts,  and\nlocations  of  a  potential seawall around the city of New York's coast-\nline.\n \n  While I agree wholeheartedly that the State must take steps  necessary\nto  address  the hazards and risks associated with climate change, State\nresources dedicated to this effort must nevertheless be committed  in  a\nprudent  manner.  There  are already several efforts underway to address\nthe specific risks associated with sea level rise, including  the  joint\neffort  of  the Department of Environmental Conservation (DEC), the Army\nCorps  of  Engineers,  the  New  Jersey  Department   of   Environmental\nProtection  and  the  New York City Office of Recovery and Resiliency to\nassess the feasibility of a range of protective strategies for  the  New\nYork  Harbor  area.  These efforts and areas of study include protective\ncoastal structures such as surge barriers, which encompass the scope  of\nthis bill. DEC is a sponsor of this project and provides funding for 25%\nof  the  entire  study  cost, which is projected to be approximately $20\nmillion.\n \n  In light of the State's existing commitment to address the same  areas\nof  study  highlighted in the bill, the creation of a new commission and\nthe concomitant obligation to hold hearings  and  develop  a  report  is\nunnecessary.  The commission that would be created by this bill would be\nredundant with several past and current efforts to understand  and  plan\nfor  hazards  associated  with  sea-level rise and coastal flooding, and\ncould divert scarce staff and funding resources from programs more like-\nly to result in tangible benefits to New Yorkers.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 338,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2018
   }
